{
  "result": "At the edge of the harbor, where salt wind tangled the gulls\u2019 cries with the clatter of masts, an old clockmaker opened his shop before sunrise. He did not repair clocks anymore. Instead, he listened to them. Each morning, he chose one silent mechanism from the shelf and held it beside the window, waiting for the town to wake. The first sounds were modest: a kettle lid, a baker\u2019s broom, a bicycle chain skipping over cobbles. Soon shutters lifted, doors sighed open, and footsteps stitched narrow streets together.\n\nHe believed each clock carried the hour it had stopped, not as a number but as a feeling: impatience, relief, grief, or the bright astonishment of arriving home.\n\nOne winter, a girl brought him a pocket watch found beneath the pier. Its hands pointed to four seventeen. \u201cThat was when my brother left,\u201d she said. \u201cCan you make it go again?\u201d\n\nThe clockmaker turned the watch in his palm. \u201cI can,\u201d he answered, \u201cbut it may not tell you what you hope.\u201d The girl nodded. Together they opened its brass back.\n\nInside, a tiny wheel had slipped loose. He set it carefully, then waited. The watch ticked once, stopped, and ticked again. The girl smiled\u2014not because time had returned, but because someone had listened to its silence.\n\nYears later, when the clockmaker\u2019s own shop fell quiet, the town gathered outside. They brought clocks that no longer worked, and set them along the windowsill. At dawn, every face caught the sunrise.",
